*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    x = !is_sd_user(username)
*
*    True if the Windows account was created by SD.
*
*       status 0, true    created by SD
*       status 0, false   exists, and SD did not create it
*       status 1, false   no such Windows account
*
*    The caller needs all three answers and they are not the same: "SD made
*    it" invites deletion, "somebody else made it" forbids it, and "it is not
*    there" is neither - it is the half-removed state DELETE.ACCOUNT exists to
*    tidy up.  Test STATUS() before believing the false.
*
*    WHY THIS QUESTION EXISTS.  CREATE.ACCOUNT USER x adopts an existing
*    Windows account when it finds one and creates a new one when it does not
*    (CREATEA lines 145-157), and it records nothing about which it did.  So
*    DELETE.ACCOUNT cannot know whether removing the Windows account undoes
*    SD's own work or deletes a login SD merely borrowed.  Under the access
*    model (PROJECT_STATUS.md 5.6) an SD account is named after a Windows user
*    who already exists, so borrowing is the ordinary case, and the borrowed
*    account may well be an administrator's.
*
*    HOW IT ANSWERS, AND WHAT THAT IS WORTH.  CREATE_USER stamps every account
*    it creates with the description "SD account".  That string is the marker
*    and it is already being written; nothing new is recorded and existing
*    accounts answer correctly without migration.
*
*    IT IS A MARKER, NOT A PROOF.  An administrator can edit a description, in
*    either direction.  The failure is deliberately one-sided: an SD-created
*    account whose description was changed is reported as not SD's, so it is
*    left in place and somebody tidies it by hand.  The opposite mistake would
*    delete a real login.  Litter is recoverable and a deleted account is not,
*    so the marker is trusted to withhold deletion and never to compel it.
*
*    LOCAL ACCOUNTS ONLY, matching !is_user and CREATE_USER.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
function is_sd_user(username)
$catalog !is_sd_user

deffun valid_os_name(name) calling '!valid_os_name'

sd_created = @false
set.status 0

if username = '' then
   set.status 1
   return sd_created
end

* The name is interpolated into the command below, so it is validated first -
* !valid_os_name permits letters, digits, dot, underscore and hyphen only.
if not(valid_os_name(username)) then
   set.status 1
   return sd_created
end

* SH1 names PowerShell, so the command IS the script.  Three exit codes,
* because the caller needs to tell "not SD's" from "not there" - see above.
* The description is compared exactly, and -ceq rather than -eq so that a
* description differing only in case is treated as somebody else's.
   ps = 'try { $u = Get-LocalUser -Name "' : username
   ps := '" -ErrorAction Stop } catch { exit 2 }; '
   ps := 'if ($u.Description -ceq "SD account") { exit 0 }; exit 1'

   os.execute ps

   stat = os.error()

   begin case
      case stat = 0
         sd_created = @true
      case stat = 1
         sd_created = @false
      case 1
         * No such account, or PowerShell could not be run at all.  Both mean
         * the caller must not go on to delete anything.
         set.status 1
   end case

return sd_created
end

* END-CODE
